  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 106 PM CDT SUN OCT 11 2026\n\nThe center of vertically stacked post-tropical cyclone Isaias is\ncurrently crossing the Blue Ridge Mountains in North Carolina.\nWraparound cloudiness is currently limited to our northeast\ncounties, and will continue to clear through the afternoon. High\npressure moving in tonight will result in light winds and clear\nskies, other than a couple patches of cirrus. Some drier air will\nbe moving in just above the surface, but recent tropical rainfall\nand a shallow inversion will result in concerns for ground fog\ndevelopment overnight. Right now confidence is only high enough\nto mention patchy fog along river valleys per latest HREF and\nHRRR guidance, but this may need to be expanded depending on\nguidance trends.\n\nStrong sub-tropical ridging moves overhead through the week,\nsteering any precipitation away from the area. Well above normal\nhigh temperatures are expected with medium to high chances for\nrecord high temperatures. While humidity values will be low,\nthose engaged in Isaias recovery operations should be mindful of\nthe heat. Ensemble clusters are in fairly good agreement on a\nfrontal passage in the Friday/Saturday timeframe due to strong \nhigh pressure moving out of the Northern Plains, with little to no\nprecipitation associated with the front. However, clusters show a\nlot of spread in the amplitude of the upper trough and associated\nstrength of the front. At this time the cluster of physical \nensemble model solutions with the stronger front/coolest \ntemperatures, similar to the deterministic GFS, are in the \nminority. However, the cooler solutions do have some support from\nthe AI guidance. Regardless, conditions should start to feel more\nlike fall next weekend.\n\n32/JDavis",
